<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyTitle>Quartz.NET</AssemblyTitle>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Title>Quartz.NET</Title>
    <Description>Quartz.NET job scheduler - cron, calendar and interval triggers, clustering over a persistent job store, and Microsoft.Extensions hosting, logging, options and dependency injection throughout. The core package: everything else builds on this one.</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);cron;clustering;background-jobs;hosted-service</PackageTags>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      The claim, and what it is worth. IsAotCompatible turns on IsTrimmable and the trim, AOT and
      single-file analyzers, and every one of them was already on here: issue #3341 turned them on step
      by step so the warnings could be counted before the claim was made, rather than the other way
      round. What the property adds is the statement, so the four properties below are spelled out
      beside it rather than left implied — a reader can see what is claimed without knowing what the
      SDK does with one flag.

      Quartz produces no IL3050 at all. The last pair belonged to the options binder and went away with
      EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator below, which makes the compiler write the binding code for
      QuartzSchedulerOptions and its five siblings instead of reflecting over them at startup.

      Since 4.6 an application publishing trimmed or native AOT sees nothing reported against Quartz,
      and nothing is suppressed to get there: there is no baseline any more, and build/Build.cs fails a
      canary publish that reports any warning against a Quartz type, on every pull request, by publishing
      Quartz.Trimming.Canary natively and running a whole SQLite store out of it. A new warning is new
      reflection to fix; the header of FeatureSwitches.cs gives the order to try fixes in, and keeps the
      history of the baseline this replaced.

      Everything that used to be reported — a job type spelled as text, the persisted JOB_CLASS_NAME
      column, the quartz.* keys that name a component or set its properties by string, and job data
      coerced onto a property through TypeDescriptor — sits behind three feature switches, declared in
      FeatureSwitches.cs:

        - Quartz.TypeNameResolution.IsSupported: a type name is answered from the types the application
          registered (AddJob<T>(), AddJobType<T>(), JobBuilder.Create<T>(), TypeLoaderOptions.Map), and
          by Type.GetType only while this is on.
        - Quartz.LegacyComponentKeys.IsSupported: a flat .type key, and a component's leftover keys, are
          read only while this is on; Quartz's own spellings of a store, a thread pool, a serializer and
          SYS_PROP map to a typeof instead.
        - Quartz.JobData.IsTypeConverterCoercionSupported: ValueConverter answers the conversions job
          data needs in practice itself, exactly as their TypeConverters do, and asks TypeDescriptor
          for any other only while this is on.

      buildTransitive/net10.0/Quartz.targets, packed below, sets all three false under PublishTrimmed and
      PublishAot, which is what lets ILLink and ILCompiler remove the guarded code rather than report
      it: the tools substitute a FeatureSwitchDefinition getter only from an explicit setting. A consumer
      opts back in with QuartzTypeNameResolutionSupport, QuartzLegacyComponentKeysSupport or
      QuartzJobDataTypeConverterSupport, and then sees the IL2026 at each guarded call site, which is the
      reflection it chose to keep.

      TransientErrorDetector, which decides whether a database error is worth retrying, also reads driver
      properties by name, and is on neither list: it names each one with [DynamicDependency], so a
      trimmed application keeps them whenever the driver is in it, and the canary asks it about a busy
      SQLite database natively.

      Where the claim ends up: on SDK 10.0.400 the property writes
      [AssemblyMetadata("IsAotCompatible", "True")] into the assembly and nothing at all into the
      nuspec, which was checked by packing rather than assumed. So nuget.org shows nothing for it, and
      the audience is the analyzers and a reader of this file.
    -->
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
    <!--
      Binding is the compiler's job here, not the reflection binder's. The generator intercepts a
      Configure<TOptions>(name, section) call in this project and replaces it with generated code, so it
      has to be on where the call is written — turning it on in an application does nothing for the six
      calls in QuartzTypedOptions, and consumers get the generated binder whether or not they set it.

      Turning it off is not a quiet change: the two suppressions that used to cover those six calls are
      deleted, so the IL2026 and IL3050 come straight back as errors. ConfigurationBindingIsSourceGeneratedTest
      counts the interceptions in the built assembly as well, because that is the only place the fact is
      recorded, and Quartz.Trimming.Canary binds a scheduler out of an IConfiguration and reads every
      value back — the reflection binder passes that check trimmed and fails it natively, dropping three
      of its ten values without a word.
    -->
    <EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator>true</EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator>
    <!--
      QuartzSchedulerOptions.DefaultTimeZone is a TimeZoneInfo, which the generator cannot construct
      (SYSLIB1100), so it skips the property (SYSLIB1101); a file writes it as an id, and
      DefaultTimeZoneSetting reads that one. Neither #pragma nor a per-file .editorconfig severity reaches
      a generator's diagnostics - both were tried - so this is the one place they can be turned off. What
      they guarded is still guarded: ConfigurationBindingIsSourceGeneratedTest
      writes every member of every bound options type into configuration and reads it back, so a member
      the generator skips without a reader of its own fails it.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);SYSLIB1100;SYSLIB1101</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The analyzer, which rides inside this package rather than under an id of its own.

    A separate Quartz.Analyzers package would be a second id every consumer has to hear about and a
    permanent publishing obligation (src/QuartzShimPackage.props says what one of those costs), for
    something nobody would ever want without Quartz. Inside analyzers/dotnet/cs it arrives with the
    reference that made it relevant. An application that does not want it sets DisableQuartzAnalyzers,
    which buildTransitive/net10.0/Quartz.targets reads; ExcludeAssets="analyzers" is not enough on the
    .NET 10 SDK, which says why.

    ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" because Quartz does not call into it; OutputItemType="Analyzer"
    because Quartz should be held to its own rules, which is also what orders the two builds. The
    pack item names the artifacts-output path rather than the project reference, because the item has
    to exist at evaluation time and the reference resolves during the build.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Quartz.Analyzers/Quartz.Analyzers.csproj" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" OutputItemType="Analyzer" />
    <None Include="$(ArtifactsPath)/bin/Quartz.Analyzers/$(Configuration.ToLowerInvariant())/Quartz.Analyzers.dll" Pack="true" PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs" Visible="false" />
    <None Include="buildTransitive/net10.0/Quartz.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="buildTransitive/net10.0/" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Xml\job_scheduling_data_2_0.xsd" />
    <!--
      The create-if-missing schema, one script per dialect, generated by 'dotnet fallout
      GenerateSchema' from build/Build.DatabaseSchema.cs. Embedded rather than shipped beside the
      assembly so that a store can provision itself from a single-file or trimmed publish.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Impl\AdoJobStore\Schema\*.sql" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" />
    <!--
      The scheduler's health check. It is here rather than in Quartz.AspNetCore because it needs
      nothing from ASP.NET Core: a worker on a dotnet/runtime image referencing Quartz.AspNetCore for
      the check alone inherited that package's FrameworkReference and failed to start (issue #3532).
      The package brings HealthCheckService and IHealthChecksBuilder; the endpoint that serves a
      report over HTTP is MapHealthChecks, which stays ASP.NET Core's.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <!--
      The abstractions, not Microsoft.Extensions.Logging itself. Quartz writes log events and never
      builds the pipeline that carries them: ILogger, ILoggerFactory, ILoggerProvider, the
      [LoggerMessage] partials and NullLoggerFactory all live here, and LoggerFactory — the
      implementation, and the AddLogging() that registers it — belongs to whoever composes the
      application. So AddQuartz registers ILogger<> and no ILoggerFactory at all, and every place
      Quartz needs a factory asks the container for one and falls back if there is none (issue #3730).
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>